register(NoPrintFrame);
define
    npAlpha=1;
    npBeta=2;
    /*  CONTROL -- two action locals, NEITHER flagged noPrint.  */
    npCtl code={
        cerr "N" npRow " control  arg " argument.taG " local1 " npC1.taG:;
        npC1 = "a";
        npC2 = "b";
        };
    /*  TREATMENT -- identical, except the LAST local is flagged noPrint
        DURING the body: after saveLocalFields walked the field list
        forward, and before restoreLocalFields walks it back.  */
    npMint code={
        cerr "N" npRow " treated  arg " argument.taG " local1 " npM1.taG:;
        npM1 = "a";
        npM2 = "b";
        npM2 :. noPrinT;
        };
    npRow=0;
    npRun code={
        npRow = 1;  npCtl(npAlpha);
        npRow = 2;  npCtl(npBeta);
        npRow = 3;  npMint(npAlpha);
        npRow = 4;  npMint(npBeta);
        };
    ;
npRun();
print "NOPRINTFRAME SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

noPrintFrameT -- FLAGGING AN ACTION LOCAL `noPrint` DURING THE BODY SHIFTS
EVERY FIELD BELOW IT IN THE NEXT ACTIVATION'S FRAME RESTORE.

saveLocalFields (GroupActions.rtn:1196) walks the action's fields FORWARD and
pushes a body for each matching (isArgument || isLocal) && !noPrint.
restoreLocalFields (:747) walks BACKWARD and pops.  The pairing is purely
positional, and noPrint is IN THE FILTER -- so a flag the body sets between
the two walks removes that field from the restore set only.  Everything below
it gets the wrong body back, `*groupBody = *body` copies the tag with it, and
the corruption is PERMANENT for the life of the action.

The two arms differ by ONE statement: `npM2 :. noPrinT;`.

Rows 1-2 are the control and rows 3-4 the treatment; row 3 is clean on both
arms because the flag is not set until the first body has already run, which
is what makes row 4 the discriminator rather than row 3.

Found 2026-09-06 under IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser, where generateParse's
`codeCopy :. noPrinT;` shifted `argument` onto `conjunct` and every call after
the first refused in setParse.  The workaround there was to DECLARE codeCopy
so it is not an action local at all; the defect itself is untouched.

⚠ Note this is bear-trap #39 pointing the wrong way.  Its rule is "declare it
if and only if more than one action touches it" -- only one action touches
codeCopy, so the rule says leave it undeclared, and undeclared is exactly what
makes it corruptible once it is also flagged noPrint.
